  "canon:split": "train",
  "canon:pmid": "36850003",
  "canon:title": "TGF-beta is elevated in hyperuricemic individuals and mediates urate-induced hyperinflammatory phenotype in human mononuclear cells.",
  "canon:abstract": "BACKGROUND: Soluble urate leads to a pro-inflammatory phenotype in human monocytes characterized by increased production of IL-1beta and downregulation of IL-1 receptor antagonist, the mechanism of which remains to be fully elucidated. Previous transcriptomic data identified differential expression of genes in the transforming growth factor (TGF)-beta pathway in monocytes exposed to urate in vitro. In this study, we explore the role of TGF-beta in urate-induced hyperinflammation in peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). METHODS: TGF-beta mRNA in unstimulated PBMCs and protein levels in plasma were measured in individuals with normouricemia, hyperuricemia and gout. For in vitro validation, PBMCs of healthy volunteers were isolated and treated with a dose ranging concentration of urate for assessment of mRNA and pSMAD2. Urate and TGF-beta priming experiments were performed with three inhibitors of TGF-beta signalling: SB-505124, 5Z-7-oxozeaenol and a blocking antibody against TGF-beta receptor II. RESULTS: TGF-beta mRNA levels were elevated in gout patients compared to healthy controls. TGF-beta-LAP levels in serum were significantly higher in individuals with hyperuricemia compared to controls. In both cases, TGF-beta correlated positively to serum urate levels. In vitro, urate exposure of PBMCs did not directly induce TGF-beta but did enhance SMAD2 phosphorylation. The urate-induced pro-inflammatory phenotype of monocytes was partly reversed by blocking TGF-beta. CONCLUSIONS: TGF-beta is elevated in individuals with hyperuricemia and correlated to serum urate concentrations. In addition, the urate-induced pro-inflammatory phenotype in human monocytes is mediated by TGF-beta signalling. Future studies are warranted to explore the intracellular pathways involved and to assess the clinical significance of urate-TGF-beta relation.",
  "canon:text": "TGF-beta is elevated in hyperuricemic individuals and mediates urate-induced hyperinflammatory phenotype in human mononuclear cells. BACKGROUND: Soluble urate leads to a pro-inflammatory phenotype in human monocytes characterized by increased production of IL-1beta and downregulation of IL-1 receptor antagonist, the mechanism of which remains to be fully elucidated. Previous transcriptomic data identified differential expression of genes in the transforming growth factor (TGF)-beta pathway in monocytes exposed to urate in vitro. In this study, we explore the role of TGF-beta in urate-induced hyperinflammation in peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). METHODS: TGF-beta mRNA in unstimulated PBMCs and protein levels in plasma were measured in individuals with normouricemia, hyperuricemia and gout. For in vitro validation, PBMCs of healthy volunteers were isolated and treated with a dose ranging concentration of urate for assessment of mRNA and pSMAD2. Urate and TGF-beta priming experiments were performed with three inhibitors of TGF-beta signalling: SB-505124, 5Z-7-oxozeaenol and a blocking antibody against TGF-beta receptor II. RESULTS: TGF-beta mRNA levels were elevated in gout patients compared to healthy controls. TGF-beta-LAP levels in serum were significantly higher in individuals with hyperuricemia compared to controls. In both cases, TGF-beta correlated positively to serum urate levels. In vitro, urate exposure of PBMCs did not directly induce TGF-beta but did enhance SMAD2 phosphorylation. The urate-induced pro-inflammatory phenotype of monocytes was partly reversed by blocking TGF-beta. CONCLUSIONS: TGF-beta is elevated in individuals with hyperuricemia and correlated to serum urate concentrations. In addition, the urate-induced pro-inflammatory phenotype in human monocytes is mediated by TGF-beta signalling. Future studies are warranted to explore the intracellular pathways involved and to assess the clinical significance of urate-TGF-beta relation.",
